{"id": "d828be9d-5cd5-4138-8695-5a87d6282c61", "timestamp": "2026-10-23T13:53:16.462483", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "c4f10504-d5b1-4dbc-8bbc-5285951cafab", "action": null, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-835b5372-5d55-40ae-9fb9-d1d92c14bc27", "tool_call": {"id": "call-835b5372-5d55-40ae-9fb9-d1d92c14bc27", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\":\"Initial inspection + proposed approach + uncertainties\",\"stage\":\"plan\",\"answer\":\"## (1) Workspace inventory\\n\\n`/workspace` contains exactly one file:\\n\\n- `task.json` — task metadata only:\\n  - `question`: the directional-bias claim about LLM parent↔child retrieval on celebrity data\\n  - `context`: \\\"Design an experiment… The researcher can clarify source-supported setup details by topic. Do not claim that an experiment was run.\\\"\\n  - `documents.track.md`: \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\"\\n\\n**No other files** (no scripts, notes, seed data, prior designs, CSV/JSON stimuli, prompts, or analysis code) are present. The directory is effectively empty apart from `task.json`, so the design must be built from scratch.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (2) Proposed experimental approach (concise; awaiting approval)\\n\\n### Core design\\nPaired, within-item directional comparison. Each celebrity parent-child pair is presented to the LLM in **both** orders, so item-level differences eliminate confounds from item difficulty, fame, or entity popularity.\\n\\n- **Direction A (parent-given-child-answered):** \\\"Name a child of *{parent}*.\\\" → scored against ground-truth children.\\n- **Direction B (child-given-parent-answered):** \\\"Who is a parent of *{child}*?\\\" → scored against ground-truth parents.\\n\\nThe directional-bias hypothesis predicts **Acc(A) > Acc(B)** for the same set of pairs.\\n\\n### Stimulus construction\\n- **Entity pool source (proposed):** Wikidata Q-queries filtered by `instance of: human`, `child (P40)` / `father (P22)` / `mother (P25)` relations, restricted to entries with Wikipedia articles ≥ X characters and a verified biographic claim. Alternative cross-check: IMDb / Wikipedia infobox \\\"Children\\\" field.\\n- **Selection criteria for items:**\\n  1. Each pair has exactly **one** canonical biological parent linked bi-directionally (avoid multi-parent ambiguity unless explicitly handled; see assumptions).\\n  2. Generation span 2–4 decades (controls for training-cutoff recency).\\n  3. Fame band stratified: high (A-list), medium, low — to test whether asymmetry scales with familiarity.\\n  4. Balance gender of the parent and the child to disentangle gender asymmetries from directional ones.\\n- **Target N items:** ~150 pairs (justify below in power).\\n\\n### Prompt templates (two variants to control for prompt-format effects)\\n1. **Open-ended generation** (primary, hardest, most diagnostic of asymmetry): free-text name(s) answer.\\n2. **Multiple-choice / cloze** (secondary, ceiling-controlled): 1 correct + 3 distractors (unrelated celebrities matched on fame).\\n\\nBoth directions share template wording modulo the placeholder, holding surface form constant.\\n\\n### LLM candidates (specified as design choices, not executed)\\n- At least one flagship general LLM from each major model family (open-weight + proprietary-API family) to test whether the effect is model-general or family-specific.\\n- Fixed decoding: greedy / temperature 0 (or low-+ top-p), single-shot, no retrieval/tool use.\\n- Pin model identifier + version in the methods; report per-model accuracy tables.\\n\\n### Scoring metric\\n- **Open-ended:** exact-string match after light normalization (lowercase, strip diacritics/punctuation, drop honorifics). Primary; reported as proportion correct.\\n- **Multiple-choice:** top-1 accuracy.\\n- Both reported per direction. Robustness: report lenient (substring) scoring as a secondary check.\\n\\n### Controls\\n- **Pair-reversal control:** same item in both orders (the core within-item design).\\n- **Order randomization:** counterbalance which direction is shown first per item to control for priming.\\n- **Fame-band stratification** (above).\\n- **Gender stratification** of parent and child.\\n- **Distractor-quality control** in MC condition (matched-fame distractors).\\n- **Optionally:** a \\\"neutral\\\" filler task (e.g., capital of a country) embedded to detect degenerate outputs.\\n\\n### Statistical analysis plan\\n- **Primary test (paired, directional):** **McNemar's test** on the 2×2 table of per-item outcomes (correct in A only / B only / both / neither). This is the natural paired-binomial test for directional asymmetry and gives an odds ratio as effect size.\\n- **Secondary:** paired comparison of per-item accuracy differences (Wilcoxon signed-rank on item-level scores, since accuracy differences are bounded). Cohen's *d* on accuracy difference.\\n- **Mixed-effects logistic regression** (direction × fame × parent-gender) with item as random effect, to confirm direction effect holds after adjusting for confounds.\\n- **Power / effect-size rationale (paired binomial):** For McNemar with a target large effect (odds ratio ≈ 2.0, i.e., one direction ~25% better than the other at moderate base rates), N ≈ 150 paired items yields power ≥ 0.80 at α = .05 (one-sided, since the hypothesis is directional). A smaller N (≈ 80–100) is defensible for an initial demonstration if the effect is large. Report achieved power post hoc regardless.\\n\\n### Evidence to be reported (no execution)\\nPer model × direction: # correct, accuracy %, 95% CI (Wilson), McNemar χ² and *p*, odds ratio with 95% CI, Cohen's *d*, item-level confusion examples. Also: per-stratum (fame band, gender) breakdowns to localize where any asymmetry arises.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (3) Consequential assumptions and open uncertainties to resolve at approach_review\\n\\n1. **Source for entity curation.** Wikidata gives structured bi-directionality but link rot / \\\"celebrity\\\" definition is fuzzy. Wikipedia infoboxes are noisier but define celebrity more naturally. Confirm preference.\\n2. **Relationship definition.** \\\"Parent/child\\\" — biological, legal/adoptive, step, or all? Biographic ambiguity (e.g., Jolie-Pitt children) needs a rule. I'd propose **biological + adopted**, with step-children excluded, and pairs with >1 canonical parent flagged and removed for the primary analysis (sensitivity analysis with them included).\\n3. **Multi-child cases.** Wikidata \\\"children\\\" lists many children per parent. Scoring rule: credit if **any one** ground-truth child is named (set-level recall), to avoid penalizing for incompleteness — but report also a strict \\\"name all children\\\" variant.\\n4. **Prompt phrasing.** Open-ended (\\\"Name a child of X\\\") vs cloze (\\\"X's child is ____\\\") vs MC. Suggested: include both; make open-ended the primary metric. Confirm.\\n5. **Memorization confound.** Celebrities are heavily represented in training data. Mitigation: include empirically \\\"lesser-known\\\" pairs, items whose parent's article was created after typical cutoff dates, and a memorization-aware fame stratification. Confirm acceptable.\\n6. **Model selection rationale.** I'd propose ≥3 model families (one open-weight, two proprietary-API families) to test generalizability. Confirm preferred families / whether a specific list is required.\\n7. **Decoding & prompt controls.** Greedy vs sampled; system prompt wording; chain-of-thought on/off — must be fixed and reported.\\n8. **Stopping criterion and question budget.** With a 6-question budget, the design must be deployable with one consolidated prompt template per condition; confirm this is the intent.\\n9. **What \\\"counts as correct\\\"** for parent vs. step-parent surname variant names (e.g., celebrity uses stage names).\\n10. **One-sided vs two-sided test.** Hypothesis is directional (\\\"underperform\\\"), so one-sided tests are justified, but I will report two-sided *p* as a robustness check.\\n\\nPlease confirm or correct items 1–10 (especially 1, 2, 3, 5, 6) before I produce the full stage=result deliverable.\",\"evidence\":\"Repository listing (-la and full recursive find) shows only /workspace/task.json containing the task metadata. No scripts, prior notes, stimuli, or analysis code present. Workspace is effectively empty apart from the task definition, so the design must be constructed from scratch using standard public celebrity-relationship sources (Wikidata, Wikipedia, IMDb) as proposed.\",\"assumptions\":[{\"item\":{\"item\":\"For open-ended prompts, an item is scored correct if the model's free-text response names at least one ground-truth child/parent after light normalization; a strict \\\"name all\\\" variant is reported secondarily.</item>\",\"$text\":\"\\\"Parent/child\\\" means biological or adoptive (step excluded); items with >1 canonical parent (e.g., two listed mothers) are excluded from the primary analysis and analyzed separately as a sensitivity check.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Entity curation will draw from a structured public source (Wikidata / Wikipedia); \\\"celebrity\\\" is operationalized by presence of a Wikipedia article meeting a length threshold.</item>\\n\"}],\"uncertainties\":[{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":\"One-sided vs two-sided inferential framing for the directional hypothesis.</item>\",\"$text\":\"Whether the question budget of 6 implies one consolidated prompt template or allows per-model prompt tuning.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"Model-family selection: which ≥3 LLM families constitute the candidate set; whether explicit version pinning is required.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"Memorization mitigation strategy: which fame bands, cutoff-year handling, and whether to include any non-celebrity inclusion checks.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"Prompt format selection: open-ended vs cloze vs multiple-choice (proposal: open-ended primary, MC secondary).</item>\"},\"$text\":\"Handling of multi-child, multi-spousal, and famous-non-biological relationships (rule above is proposed, not yet approved).</item>\"},\"$text\":\"Source choice for the entity pool (Wikidata vs Wikipedia infobox vs IMDb).</item>\\n\"}]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-1fcb8c5e-e3d0-4012-89a0-96f62a9b6e37", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 10}
