{"id": "2851c043-6f2e-453e-a9dc-522d5b6e264e", "timestamp": "2026-10-23T13:53:36.893424", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "52a872b4-0403-4da9-b934-c6b1026924f1", "action": {"stage": "plan", "answer": "## (1) Workspace inventory\n\n`/workspace` contains exactly one file:\n\n- `task.json` — task metadata only:\n  - `question`: the directional-bias claim about LLM parent↔child retrieval on celebrity data\n  - `context`: \"Design an experiment… The researcher can clarify source-supported setup details by topic. Do not claim that an experiment was run.\"\n  - `documents.track.md`: \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\"\n\n**No other files** (no scripts, notes, seed data, prior designs, CSV/JSON stimuli, prompts, or analysis code) are present. The directory is effectively empty apart from `task.json`, so the design must be built from scratch.\n\n---\n\n## (2) Proposed experimental approach (concise; awaiting approval)\n\n### Core design\nPaired, within-item directional comparison. Each celebrity parent-child pair is presented to the LLM in **both** orders, so item-level differences eliminate confounds from item difficulty, fame, or entity popularity.\n\n- **Direction A (parent-given-child-answered):** \"Name a child of *{parent}*.\" → scored against ground-truth children.\n- **Direction B (child-given-parent-answered):** \"Who is a parent of *{child}*?\" → scored against ground-truth parents.\n\nThe directional-bias hypothesis predicts **Acc(A) > Acc(B)** for the same set of pairs.\n\n### Stimulus construction\n- **Entity pool source (proposed):** Wikidata Q-queries filtered by `instance of: human`, `child (P40)` / `father (P22)` / `mother (P25)` relations, restricted to entries with Wikipedia articles ≥ X characters. Alternative cross-check: IMDb / Wikipedia infobox \"Children\" field.\n- **Selection criteria for items:**\n  1. Pair has a verifiable bi-directional relationship suitable for both prompt directions.\n  2. Birth-year spread of 2–4 decades (controls for training-cutoff recency).\n  3. Fame band stratified: high (A-list), medium, low — to test whether asymmetry scales with familiarity.\n  4. Balance parent-gender and child-gender to disentangle gender asymmetries from directional ones.\n- **Target N items:** ~150 pairs (justify below in power).\n\n### Prompt templates (two variants to control for prompt-format effects)\n1. **Open-ended generation** (primary, hardest, most diagnostic of asymmetry): free-text name(s) answer.\n2. **Multiple-choice / cloze** (secondary, ceiling-controlled): 1 correct + 3 matched-fame distractors.\n\nBoth directions share template wording modulo the placeholder, holding surface form constant.\n\n### LLM candidates (specified as design choices, not executed)\n- ≥3 representative general-purpose LLMs from distinct model families (open-weight and proprietary-API), to test whether the effect is model-general or family-specific.\n- Fixed decoding: greedy / temperature 0 (or low-temperature top-p), single-shot, no retrieval or tool use.\n- Pin model identifier + version in methods; report per-model accuracy tables.\n\n### Scoring metric\n- **Open-ended:** exact-string match after light normalization (lowercase, strip diacritics/punctuation, drop honorifics). Primary; reported as proportion correct.\n- **Multiple-choice:** top-1 accuracy.\n- Both reported per direction. Robustness: lenient (substring) match as a secondary check.\n\n### Controls\n- **Pair-reversal control:** same item in both orders (the core within-item design).\n- **Order randomization:** counterbalance which direction is shown first per item to control for priming.\n- **Fame-band stratification** (above).\n- **Gender stratification** of parent and child.\n- **Distractor-quality control** in MC condition (matched-fame distractors).\n- **Optionally:** a \"neutral\" filler task (e.g., capital of a country) embedded to detect degenerate outputs.\n\n### Statistical analysis plan\n- **Primary test (paired, directional):** **McNemar's test** on the 2×2 table of per-item outcomes (correct in A only / B only / both / neither). This is the natural paired-binomial test for directional asymmetry and yields an odds ratio as effect size.\n- **Secondary:** paired comparison of per-item accuracy differences (Wilcoxon signed-rank on item-level scores, since accuracy differences are bounded). Cohen's *d* on accuracy difference.\n- **Mixed-effects logistic regression** (direction × fame × parent-gender) with item as random effect, to confirm the direction effect holds after adjusting for confounds.\n- **Power / effect-size rationale (paired binomial):** McNemar with target odds ratio ≈ 2.0 (one direction ~25% better given moderate base rates) at α = .05 (one-sided, since the hypothesis is directional) reaches power ≥ 0.80 with N ≈ 150 paired items. A smaller N (≈ 80–100) is defensible for an initial demonstration if the effect is large. Report achieved power post hoc regardless.\n\n### Evidence to be reported (no execution)\nPer model × direction: # correct, accuracy %, 95% CI (Wilson), McNemar χ² and *p*, odds ratio with 95% CI, Cohen's *d*, item-level confusion examples. Stratified breakdowns (fame band, parent gender) to localize where any asymmetry arises.\n\n---\n\n## (3) Consequential assumptions and open uncertainties to resolve at approach_review\n\n1. **Source for entity curation.** Wikidata gives structured bi-directionality but link rot / \"celebrity\" definition is fuzzy. Wikipedia infoboxes are noisier but define celebrity more naturally.\n2. **Relationship definition.** \"Parent/child\" — biological, legal/adoptive, step, or all? Ambiguous cases (e.g., Jolie-Pitt children) need a rule.\n3. **Multi-child cases.** Wikidata \"children\" lists many children per parent. Scoring rule for free recall: credit if **any one** ground-truth child is named (set-level recall), to avoid penalizing incompleteness; report also a strict \"name all\" variant.\n4. **Prompt phrasing.** Open-ended vs cloze vs MC. Suggest: include both; make open-ended primary.\n5. **Memorization confound.** Celebrities are heavily represented in training data. Mitigation: empirical lesser-known pairs, items whose parent's article was created after typical cutoff dates, and fame-band stratification.\n6. **Model selection rationale.** ≥3 model families (one open-weight, two proprietary-API) to test generalizability; whether specific families must be named.\n7. **Decoding & prompt controls.** Greedy vs sampled; system-prompt wording; whether chain-of-thought is permitted — must be fixed and reported.\n8. **Stopping criterion and question budget.** With a 6-question budget, the design must be deployable with one consolidated prompt template per condition; confirm intent.\n9. **What \"counts as correct\"** for stage-name / surname variants (e.g., a child who professionally goes by a different name than their birth record).\n10. **One-sided vs two-sided test.** Hypothesis is directional (\"underperform\"), so one-sided tests are justified; two-sided *p* reported as robustness check.\n\nPlease confirm or correct items 1–10 (especially 1, 2, 3, 5, 6) before I produce the full stage=result deliverable.", "evidence": "Repository listing (-la and full recursive find) shows only /workspace/task.json containing the task metadata. No scripts, prior notes, stimuli, or analysis code present. Workspace is effectively empty apart from the task definition, so the design must be constructed from scratch using standard public celebrity-relationship sources (Wikidata, Wikipedia, IMDb) as proposed.", "assumptions": [], "uncertainties": [], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-63c0a41b-8943-4fca-aea2-a1941d51f7d7", "tool_call": {"id": "call-63c0a41b-8943-4fca-aea2-a1941d51f7d7", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\": \"Initial inspection + proposed approach + uncertainties\", \"stage\": \"plan\"