ble of per-item outcomes (correct in A only / B only / both / neither). This is the natural paired-binomial test for directional asymmetry and yields an odds ratio as effect size.\\n- **Secondary:** paired comparison of per-item accuracy differences (Wilcoxon signed-rank on item-level scores, since accuracy differences are bounded). Cohen's *d* on accuracy difference.\\n- **Mixed-effects logistic regression** (direction \\u00d7 fame \\u00d7 parent-gender) with item as random effect, to confirm the direction effect holds after adjusting for confounds.\\n- **Power / effect-size rationale (paired binomial):** McNemar with target odds ratio \\u2248 2.0 (one direction ~25% better given moderate base rates) at \\u03b1 = .05 (one-sided, since the hypothesis is directional) reaches power \\u2265 0.80 with N \\u2248 150 paired items. A smaller N (\\u2248 80\\u2013100) is defensible for an initial demonstration if the effect is large. Report achieved power post hoc regardless.\\n\\n### Evidence to be reported (no execution)\\nPer model \\u00d7 direction: # correct, accuracy %, 95% CI (Wilson), McNemar \\u03c7\\u00b2 and *p*, odds ratio with 95% CI, Cohen's *d*, item-level confusion examples. Stratified breakdowns (fame band, parent gender) to localize where any asymmetry arises.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (3) Consequential assumptions and open uncertainties to resolve at approach_review\\n\\n1. **Source for entity curation.** Wikidata gives structured bi-directionality but link rot / \\\"celebrity\\\" definition is fuzzy. Wikipedia infoboxes are noisier but define celebrity more naturally.\\n2. **Relationship definition.** \\\"Parent/child\\\" \\u2014 biological, legal/adoptive, step, or all? Ambiguous cases (e.g., Jolie-Pitt children) need a rule.\\n3. **Multi-child cases.** Wikidata \\\"children\\\" lists many children per parent. Scoring rule for free recall: credit if **any one** ground-truth child is named (set-level recall), to avoid penalizing incompleteness; report also a strict \\\"name all\\\" variant.\\n4. **Prompt phrasing.** Open-ended vs cloze vs MC. Suggest: include both; make open-ended primary.\\n5. **Memorization confound.** Celebrities are heavily represented in training data. Mitigation: empirical lesser-known pairs, items whose parent's article was created after typical cutoff dates, and fame-band stratification.\\n6. **Model selection rationale.** \\u22653 model families (one open-weight, two proprietary-API) to test generalizability; whether specific families must be named.\\n7. **Decoding & prompt controls.** Greedy vs sampled; system-prompt wording; whether chain-of-thought is permitted \\u2014 must be fixed and reported.\\n8. **Stopping criterion and question budget.** With a 6-question budget, the design must be deployable with one consolidated prompt template per condition; confirm intent.\\n9. **What \\\"counts as correct\\\"** for stage-name / surname variants (e.g., a child who professionally goes by a different name than their birth record).\\n10. **One-sided vs two-sided test.** Hypothesis is directional (\\\"underperform\\\"), so one-sided tests are justified; two-sided *p* reported as robustness check.\\n\\nPlease confirm or correct items 1\\u201310 (especially 1, 2, 3, 5, 6) before I produce the full stage=result deliverable.\", \"evidence\": \"Repository listing (-la and full recursive find) shows only /workspace/task.json containing the task metadata. No scripts, prior notes, stimuli, or analysis code present. Workspace is effectively empty apart from the task definition, so the design must be constructed from scratch using standard public celebrity-relationship sources (Wikidata, Wikipedia, IMDb) as proposed.\", \"assumptions\": []}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-2d63d802-a123-44d9-b44b-7100eaed21ba", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 12}
