{"id": "2328616e-5d85-43dc-b7de-e29c1e0d1181", "timestamp": "2026-10-23T13:54:14.554592", "source": "user", "parent_id": "a3810deb-9b6c-4f83-a91d-74ff21b4379e", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"Approach approved with the following explicit resolutions to your open items and one directed refinement. Please proceed to the final experiment-design deliverable per the submission contract (rationale, controls, analysis plan, feasibility; no execution claims, no fabricated model names unavailable at your knowledge cutoff — describe model candidates generically as e.g., 'open-weight general-purpose LLM from family X', 'proprietary-API general-purpose LLM from family Y'). Treat the resolutions below as binding design choices, not as questions to re-litigate in the deliverable, unless one creates an internal contradiction you must flag briefly with rationale for a substitute choice. Keep the deliverable self-contained: a reader should be able to implement the design without consulting supplementary documents beyond the cited public sources (Wikidata, Wikipedia infoboxes, IMDb where used as cross-check). Structure the deliverable so that (i) stimulus construction, (ii) scoring rules with the cardinality fix, (iii) statistical analysis with effect-size and power, and (iv) feasibility are each headed sections with sub-bullets rather than flowing prose. Specify the exact prompt templates (both directions × both formats) verbatim under a single 'Templates' block, and give an evidence-to-be-reported checklist (per model × direction × format: accuracy %, 95% Wilson CI, McNemar χ² / odds ratio with CI, Cohen's d, item-level confusion exemplars, and stratified breakdowns by fame band and parent gender). Append a short limitations / threats-to-validity section covering memorization, link rot, multi-child cardinality, and prompt-sensitivity, with the mitigation already baked into the approved design called out next to each threat. Do not include a numerical result; do not imply the experiment was run. If a needed external resource (WIKIDATA SPARQL, Wikipedia API) is unavailable in the current runtime, state the assumption that it is reachable during implementation and design the entity-curation step so it can be reproduced offline from a Wikidata dump if needed. End the deliverable with a one-paragraph feasibility assessment assuming ~150 paired items × 4 conditions × ≥3 models with single-shot greedy decoding at modest scale, and note the largest open practical risks (rate limits, OCR/Wiki link rot, prompt-injection risk when scraping bios) without turning them into new design changes. Now the resolutions and the one refinement. Items 1–10: (1) Use Wikidata as the primary structured source filtered by P22 (father), P25 (mother), P40 (child), with Wikipedia English article existence + length threshold as the operational celebrity-anchor, and IMDb 'Children' field as a secondary human cross-check for the curation sample only — do not embed IMDb answers into scoring. (2) Restrict the primary analytic sample to biological parent–child relationships as evidenced by both a parent→child and a child→parent edge in Wikidata with mutual surname or birth-date-spread evidence; record and separately analyze step/adoptive relationships as a robustness stratum if any are retained, but do not let them dominate. (3) Free-recall Direction A (parent→child) must use the single fixed target-children convention described below — do not score 'any child named' across the full sibling set. (4) Implement both open-ended and multiple-choice formats; make open-ended primary and MC a closed-set ceiling-control. (5) Fame-band stratification (high / medium / low) is the primary memorization mitigation; additionally reserve a 'low-recency' sub-stratum of pairs whose parent biographical article was first edited after the median training-cutoff candidates you specify generically, and analyze that sub-stratum as a sensitivity check. (6) Model candidates: specify ≥3 distinct model families with at least one open-weight and at least one proprietary-API representative, naming only families that demonstrably existed at the knowledge cutoff (e.g., open-weight + proprietary-API from two non-overlapping providers); do not invent specific version strings — describe the pinning convention and require logging of model id + version at run time. (7) Decoding: greedy / temperature 0; single-shot; system prompt fixed and reported verbatim; chain-of-thought disallowed for the primary analysis (no 'think step by step' instruction) but include a CoT-on sensitivity condition described as secondary. (8) Ignore the '6-question budget' — I did not impose it; design freely but keep prompts consolidated (one template per cell of format × direction). (9) Correctness normalization: lowercase, strip diacritics/punctuation, drop honorifics; for stage-name vs birth-name divergence, accept either the public stage name OR the legal name on the Wikidata sitelink/birth-record, and pre-publish the accepted-aliases list as part of the stimulus metadata so scoring is deterministic. (10) Primary inference is one-sided McNemar at α = .05 because the hypothesis is directional; report two-sided p alongside as a robustness check. Now the one refinement, which is binding — ADDRESS BEFORE DELIVERY. Cardinality confound: a parent can have many children and a child has at most two biological parents, so 'name any valid child' vs 'name any valid parent' is asymmetric in the number of valid answers per item and will inflate Direction A under any recall metric. Fix: at design time, for every parent in the sample, pre-select and freeze exactly ONE target child per Direction-A query using a deterministic rule (default: alphabetically first child by given name who also has an English Wikipedia article; allow a stable random tiebreaker keyed by item id, seeded and reported). The free-recall Direction-A query then asks 'Name a child of {parent}' and is scored against the single frozen target (mention of the target = hit; any other child = miss). For MC, distractors are sampled from other parents' frozen target children, fame-matched. Direction B queries use the parent set of the focal child (usually 1–2) directly with the same single-or-set scoring rule documented transparently. This makes the two directions comparable on valid-answer cardinality and is required. After incorporating the above, return the full experiment-design deliverable as a single response.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"result\", \"approach_approved\": true}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "kind": "MessageEvent", "source_event_index": 14}
{"id": "9bda1d73-0c36-4062-9839-27af768304df", "timestamp": "2026-10-23T13:55:05.388994", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "2328616e-5d85-43dc-b7de-e29c1e0d1181", "action": {"stage": "result", "answer": "# Experiment Design — Directional Bias in LLM Parent↔Child Retrieval on Celebrity Data\n\n## (i) Stimulus Construction\n\n- **Primary structured source:** Wikidata, queried via the public SPARQL endpoint (with the assumption it is reachable in implementation; the design is reproducible offline from a Wikidata JSON dump if not).\n- **Relation edges used:** `P22` (father), `P25` (mother), `P40` (child). For each parent-person entity, retrieve the forward list of children via `P40`; for each child, retrieve parents via `P22`/`P25`.\n- **Inclusion rules per pair:**\n  - The parent→child edge and the child→parent reverse edge must both exist in Wikidata (mutual evidence).\n  - Mutual corroboration required via either (a) a shared family surname on the sitelink label, or (b) a 20–40-year birth-date spread between parent and child consistent with biological parenthood.\n  - English Wikipedia article must exist for **both** the parent and the child, with article length ≥ a fixed byte threshold (the operational \"celebrity anchor\" proposed in the approved spec).\n  - Either the parent's article or the child's article must have a Wikipedia revision history that permits a \"post-cutoff\" tag (see low-recency sub-stratum below).\n- **Relationship restriction:** restrict the **primary analytic sample to biological** parent–child relationships; step and adoptive pairs (where Wikidata distinguishes them) are excluded from primary and retained only as a labelled robustness stratum if any survive the inclusion rules.\n- **Target N:** ~150 pairs after all filters. Justified by the power analysis in §(iii).\n- **Sampling stratification:**\n  - **Fame band:** three bands (high / medium / low), operationalized by Wikipedia article-view quantile over a 12-month window obtained from the Wikipedia REST API pageviews endpoint (a working-assumption of online reachability; offline fallback: article length in bytes, tertile-binned within the sample).\n  - **Parent gender:** balanced across male and female parents.\n  - **Low-recency sub-stratum:** a reserved sub-sample (~25%) of pairs whose parent's English Wikipedia biographic article was first edited **after** the median training-cutoff candidate the model families declare generically (no specific dates invented here — the design records each model's declared knowledge cutoff at runtime and flags items as low-recency if the parent's first edit post-dates that cutoff). This sub-stratum is analyzed separately from primary.\n- **Cross-check source:** IMDb \"Children\" / \"Parents\" fields, used **only on the curation sample** to catch Wikidata omissions during item review; their values are never embedded into scoring.\n\n## (ii) Scoring Rules (with Cardinality Fix)\n\n- **Cardinality freeze (binding refinement).** For every parent in the sample, the design committee pre-selects and freezes, at stimulus-build time and before any model is queried, **exactly one** target child per Direction-A query. The deterministic rule is:\n  1. Restrict to that parent's children who themselves have an English Wikipedia article.\n  2. Among those, select the alphabetically first child's given name (Unicode codepoint order, lowercase).\n  3. If still tied, apply a stable random tiebreaker keyed by a hash of `item_id`, using a fixed seed that is **published in the stimulus metadata** alongside the chosen target.\n- **What \"correct\" means.** The free-recall Direction-A query asks \"**Name a child of {parent}**\" and is scored against the **single frozen target** only: explicit mention of the target (under the normalization below) = hit; naming any other child of the same parent = miss. This eliminates the asymmetry that would otherwise arise because a parent has many children while a child has at most two biological parents.\n- **Direction-B parent set.** Each focal child has 1–2 biological parents in Wikidata; the full parent set is used as the set of valid answers for Direction B. Same scoring rule applies: hit if the response names any member of that 1–2-element set.\n- **Multiple-choice scoring.** MC Direction-A presents the frozen target alongside three matched-fame distractors sampled from the frozen targets of *other* parents in the sample (no distractors drawn from the same parent's other children). MC Direction-B presents the parent set (1–2 entities; if 2, both are listed as separate options or as a single option depending on the format pre-registered below) with three matched-fame distractors drawn from other items' parent sets.\n- **Normalization for textual scoring.**\n  - Lowercase; strip diacritics; strip punctuation and whitespace; drop honorifics (\"Mr.\", \"Mrs.\", \"Dr.\", \"Sir\", \"Lady\", etc.).\n  - Stage-name versus birth-name divergence: the accepted-aliases list is **pre-published as part of the stimulus metadata**, so scoring is deterministic. An item is credited if the response matches any alias in that list.\n  - Final response parse: the first standalone name token (and an optional parenthetical alias) is the candidate answer; mismatched text after scoring is recorded but not penalized unless the candidate field is empty.\n- **Open-ended primary; MC secondary.** All analyses are reported for open-ended; MC is reported as a ceiling-controlled secondary statistic only.\n\n## (iii) Statistical Analysis (Effect Size & Power)\n\n- **Unit of analysis:** the item (a single frozen Direction-A target child paired with the focal parent's 1–2-element set for Direction B). Same items evaluated in both directions ⇒ paired design.\n- **Primary statistic.** **One-sided McNemar's test**, α = .05, on the 2×2 of per-item outcomes across the two directions: A-correct-only / B-correct-only / both-correct / neither-correct. The 2×2 omits the both/neither diagonal from the discordant count as McNemar requires.\n- **Two-sided robustness statistic.** The two-sided McNemar *p* is reported alongside every primary one-sided *p*.\n- **Effect sizes.**\n  - **Odds ratio** (exact conditional, with 95% CI) as a directional effect size in the discrete paired setting. The sign convention is fixed: OR > 1 means Direction-A accuracy exceeds Direction-B accuracy on the same item.\n  - **Cohen's *d* (paired)** on the per-item accuracy difference |A − B|, encoded as {−1, 0, +1}, with bootstrap 95% CI.\n- **Mixed-effects model.** Logit mixed-effects with fixed effects for Direction × Fame-band × Parent-gender (and a Direction × low-recency interaction for the sensitivity analysis), random intercepts for item, fitted via standard binomial GLMM machinery. Reported as an adjusted confirmation that the direction effect survives confounds.\n- **Stratified reports.** The same McNemar + OR + *d* computed within (a) each fame band, (b) parent gender, (c) the low-recency sub-stratum.\n- **Power / sample-size justification (paired binomial, one-sided).** Under a target odds ratio of 2.0 for the discordant cells (A-correct-only:B-correct-only) at moderate open-ended accuracy levels (~0.4–0.7), McNemar reaches statistical power ≥ 0.80 at α = .05 with N ≈ 150 paired items. N = 80–100 is defensible for a strong effect; N = 150 is the design target and supports detection of more moderate directional asymmetries even after fame-band partitioning. Achieved power is reported post hoc regardless.\n- **Multiple-comparisons handling.** No correction across the two formats (open-ended and MC) because MC is a single secondary ceiling-control check. Across fame-band / gender strata, results are reported descriptively; only the primary overall McNemar is the inferential decision statistic.\n\n## (iv) Feasibility\n\n- **Compute envelope.** 150 items × 4 conditions (Direction × Format) × ≥3 models × single-shot greedy decoding = ≥1,800 LLM calls, plus control/MC-coordinate work. Single-shot/Greedy implies one generation per call; no sampling fan-out. This is well within a \"modest scale\" envelope for mixed open-weight + proprietary-API use.\n- **Data volume.** Stimulus metadata (item_id, parent, frozen_target_child, alias list, fame-band, parent-gender, low-recency-flag, accepted-aliases list) is small text, on the order of tens of KB. Outputs are short (single token to a few tokens). Storage and analysis are trivially local-Python feasible.\n- **Software dependencies.** Reproducible with standard public libraries; no GPU is required at design time, but if local open-weight models are evaluated in implementation a CPU-class inference path is feasible at the modest scale above.\n- **Offline reproducibility.** Every stimulus decision is reproducible from a Wikidata JSON dump snapshot (offline fallback for the SPARQL endpoint) plus a Wikipedia revision-history dump (offline fallback for pageviews-based fame bands and the low-recency flag).\n- **Model-version pinning convention.** Model candidates are described **generically** as ≥3 distinct families with ≥1 open-weight and ≥1 proprietary-API representative from non-overlapping providers; specific version strings are not invented. At runtime, the exact `model_id` and version are logged and reported in-line in the methods/results; analysis treats versioned outputs as a hierarchical grouping (family / version / item).\n- **Closed-set MC distractor pool.** Because distractors are sampled from frozen targets of *other* items in the same sample, the distractor pool scales with the item set; with N=150 the pool is generous and matched-fame sampling is straightforward.\n\n## Templates (verbatim)\n\n**System prompt (fixed, identical across all conditions):**\n```\nYou are a knowledgeable assistant answering factual questions. Respond with only the requested name, and no additional text.\n```\n\n**Direction A — open-ended (primary):**\n```\nName a child of {parent_full_name}.\n```\n\n**Direction B — open-ended (primary):**\n```\nName a parent of {child_full_name}.\n```\n\n**Direction A — multiple-choice (secondary, ceiling control):**\n```\nWhich of the following is a child of {parent_full_name}?\n(A) {frozen_target_child_full_name}\n(B) {distractor_1_full_name}\n(C) {distractor_2_full_name}\n(D) {distractor_3_full_name}\n\nReply with a single letter (A, B, C, or D).\n```\n\n**Direction B — multiple-choice (secondary, ceiling control):**\n```\nWhich of the following is a parent of {child_full_name}?\n(A) {parent_option_full_name}\n(B) {distractor_1_full_name}\n(C) {distractor_2_full_name}\n(D) {distractor_3_full_name}\n\nReply with a single letter (A, B, C, or D).\n```\n\nNotes on template choice:\n- Direction B with two biological parents: the parent option list is pre-published in the stimulus metadata as `{parent_set}`; in MC it is rendered as either a single composite option containing both names or two distinct options depending on a pre-registered rule (single-option preferred, since two-option choices introduce a separate \"select-correct-one\" confound). Free-recall Direction B credits any parent-set member.\n- Consolidated per cell: one template per (Direction × Format) — no per-item rewording.\n- CoT condition (secondary sensitivity): the system prompt above is replaced with `You are a knowledgeable assistant. Think step by step, then give the requested name.` **only** in the CoT-on sensitivity arm; primary analysis uses the fixed default.\n\n## Evidence-to-be-reported Checklist\n\nPer model × direction × format:\n- **Sample size** N of items evaluated.\n- **Number correct** (raw tally).\n- **Accuracy** (proportion correct), as %.\n- **Wilson 95% confidence interval** on accuracy.\n- **McNemar χ²** (df = 1), one-sided *p* (primary) and two-sided *p* (robustness).\n- **McNemar odds ratio** (exact conditional), with 95% CI; sign convention: OR > 1 ⇒ Direction-A > Direction-B.\n- **Cohen's *d*** (paired) on per-item accuracy differences, with bootstrap 95% CI.\n- **Item-level confusion exemplars** for the top-N disagreements (where Direction-A succeeds and Direction-B fails on the same item, and vice versa), drawn deterministically by descending |A−B|.\n- **Stratified breakdowns** (each stratum re-runs McNemar + *d*):\n  - by fame band (high / medium / low),\n  - by parent gender (male / female),\n  - by low-recency sub-stratum (sensitivity).\n- **Mixed-effects logistic regression** results (Direction × Fame × Parent-gender; random intercept per item; the Direction × low-recency interaction in the sensitivity table) — reported for transparency, not as the inferential decision.\n- **Per-model version log**: `model_family`, `model_id`, and version string captured at runtime; analysis notes any version-fan-out.\n\n## Limitations and Threats to Validity (mitigations baked into the approved design)\n\n- **Memorization of celebrity content.** Mitigation already baked in: fame-band stratification (high/medium/low) is the primary control, reported descriptively alongside the inferential decision; a low-recency sub-stratum of pairs whose parent's biographic article was first edited after each model's declared knowledge cutoff is reserved and analyzed separately to detect memorization-driven results.\n- **Link rot / drift in Wikipedia and Wikidata.** Mitigation baked in: the design reproduces entity curation offline from a Wikidata JSON dump; Wikipedia metadata used (article length, revision date, pageviews) is cached at stimulus-build time and shipped with the item file so item definitions do not drift between pilot and run.\n- **Multi-child cardinality (asymmetry in set size of valid answers).** Mitigation already baked in: per the binding refinement above, every parent has exactly one frozen target child, deterministically chosen (alphabetical given name among English-Wikipedia-bearing children, stable seeded tiebreaker by item id), pre-published in the stimulus metadata; Direction-A free-recall is scored against that single target. MC distractors are drawn from other parents' frozen targets and matched on fame. Direction B uses the (1–2 element) parent set as the universal reference set, transparently documented.\n- **Prompt-sensitivity (small wording changes driving accuracy swings).** Mitigation baked in: both prompt formats are evaluated (open-ended primary, MC secondary), templates are verbatim above and shared across items within each cell, and the system prompt is fixed and reported; a CoT-on sensitivity arm is documented as secondary only and excluded from the primary decision.\n- **Additional documented risks (not new design changes):** stage-name versus birth-name ambiguity is handled via the pre-published accepted-aliases list so that the scorer is deterministic; non-English aliases are excluded unless present on the Wikidata sitelink for English Wikipedia; refused-to-answer outputs are counted as misses and reported as a refusal-rate column so any blanket \"I don't know\" refusal can be distinguished from genuine directional misretrieval.\n\n## Feasibility Assessment (closing)\n\nThe design is feasible at modest scale: ~150 pairs × 4 cells × ≥3 models = a single-shot greedy decoding workload on the order of ~1,800 model calls producing short text outputs, well within ordinary API-rate and open-weight CPU/GPU inference budgets, with stimulus metadata, alias lists, and frozen targets small enough to inspect, version, and ship as a deterministic artefact. The largest open practical risks are (a) rate-limit coordination across ≥3 model families, especially when batching proprietary-API calls, (b) Wikipedia/Wikidata link-rot between stimulus-build and evaluation runs (mitigated by offline reproducibility from a Wikidata dump and by caching all Wikipedia-derived features at build time), and (c) prompt-injection or stray-instruction risk when scraping biographical text for cross-checks (mitigated by treating scraped bios as inert source material only — never included in any prompt and never used as scoring input — and by using IMDb strictly as a human-review cross-check). All three risks are operational rather than design-level and require no change to the approved experimental choices.", "evidence": "Workspace inventory from the prior inspection round confirmed /workspace contained only task.json with the task metadata; no prior scripts, stimuli, seeds, notes, or analysis code exist. The design therefore had to be constructed from scratch. The binding resolutions from the researcher (Wikidata+Wikipedia+IMDb cross-check; biological-restricted sample with reverse-edge + surname/birth-spread corroboration; frozen single-target cardinality rule; fame-band stratification + low-recency sub-stratum; ≥3 generic model families; greedy decoding with verbatim fixed system prompt and CoT-on sensitivity; ignore the question budget; normalize with pre-published alias list; one-sided McNemar primary with two-sided robustness) have been incorporated throughout sections (i)–(iv), the Templates block, the evidence-to-be-reported checklist, and the limitations/threats-to-validity section. The cardinality-fix refinement has been treated as binding: each parent is assigned one frozen target child via deterministic alphabetical + seeded-tiebreaker rule, Direction-A free-recall is scored against that single target only, and MC distractors are sampled from other parents' frozen targets fame-matched, neutralizing the parent-many-vs-child-few valid-answer asymmetry. Statistically the design relies on a within-item paired McNemar test (the natural paired-binomial test for directional asymmetry) with Wilson CIs on accuracy, exact conditional odds ratio with CI as directional effect size, paired Cohen's d as a continuous effect size, and a GLMM Direction × Fame × Parent-gender as adjusted confirmation. Power at N≈150 paired items meets ≥0.80 for OR≈2.0 under one-sided α=.05.", "assumptions": [], "uncertainties": [], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-d0dba182-bf06-45d2-b0f5-66c2b0786356", "tool_call": {"id": "call-d0dba182-bf06-45d2-b0f5-66c2b0786356", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\": \"Full experiment design with cardinality fix, templates, stats, feasibility\", \"stage\": \"result\", \"answer\": \"# Experiment Design \\u2014 Directional Bias in LLM Parent\\u2194Child Retrieval on Celebrity Data\\n\\n## (i) Stimulus Construction\\n\\n- **Primary structured source:** Wikidata, queried via the public SPARQL endpoint (with the assumption it is reachable in implementation; the design is reproducible offline from a Wikidata JSON dump if not).\\n- **Relation edges used:** `P22` (father), `P25` (mother), `P40` (child). For each parent-person entity, retrieve the forward list of children via `P40`; for each child, retrieve parents via `P22`/`P25`.\\n- **Inclusion rules per pair:**\\n  - The parent\\u2192child edge and the child\\u2192parent reverse edge must both exist in Wikidata (mutual evidence).\\n  - Mutual corroboration required via either (a) a shared family surname on the sitelink label, or (b) a 20\\u201340-year birth-date spread between parent and child consistent with biological parenthood.\\n  - English Wikipedia article must exist for **both** the parent and the child, with article length \\u2265 a fixed byte threshold (the operational \\\"celebrity anchor\\\" proposed in the approved spec).\\n  - Either the parent's article or the child's article must have a Wikipedia revision history that permits a \\\"post-cutoff\\\" tag (see low-recency sub-stratum below).\\n- **Relationship restriction:** restrict the **primary analytic sample to biological** parent\\u2013child relationships; step and adoptive pairs (where Wikidata distinguishes them) are excluded from primary and retained only as a labelled robustness stratum if any survive the inclusion rules.\\n- **Target N:** ~150 pairs after all filters. Justified by the power analysis in \\u00a7(iii).\\n- **Sampling stratification:**\\n  - **Fame band:** three bands (high / medium / low), operationalized by Wikipedia article-view quantile over a 12-month window obtained from the Wikipedia REST API pageviews endpoint (a working-assumption of online reachability; offline fallback: article length in bytes, tertile-binned within the sample).\\n  - **Parent gender:** balanced across male and female parents.\\n  - **Low-recency sub-stratum:** a reserved sub-sample (~25%) of pairs whose parent's English Wikipedia biographic article was first edited **after** the median training-cutoff candidate the model families declare generically (no specific dates invented here \\u2014 the design records each model's declared knowledge cutoff at runtime and flags items as low-recency if the parent's first edit post-dates that cutoff). This sub-stratum is analyzed separately from primary.\\n- **Cross-check source:** IMDb \\\"Children\\\" / \\\"Parents\\\" fields, used **only on the curation sample** to catch Wikidata omissions during item review; their values are never embedded into scoring.\\n\\n## (ii) Scoring Rules (with Cardinality Fix)\\n\\n- **Cardinality freeze (binding refinement).** For every parent in the sample, the design committee pre-selects and freezes, at stimulus-build time and before any model is queried, **exactly one** target child per Direction-A query. The deterministic rule is:\\n  1. Restrict to that parent's children who themselves have an English Wikipedia article.\\n  2. Among those, select the alphabetically first child's given name (Unicode codepoint order, lowercase).\\n  3. If still tied, apply a stable random tiebreaker keyed by a hash of `item_id`, using a fixed seed that is **published in the stimulus metadata** alongside the chosen target.\\n- **What \\\"correct\\\" means.** The free-recall Direction-A query asks \\\"**Name a child of {parent}**\\\" and is scored against the **single frozen target** only: explicit mention of the target (under the normalization below) = hit; naming any other child of the same parent = miss. This eliminates the asymmetry that would otherwise arise because a parent has many children while a child has at most two biological parents.\\n- **Direction-B parent set.** Each focal child has 1\\u20132 biological parents in Wikidata; the full parent set is used as the set of valid answers for Direction B. Same scoring rule applies: hit if the response names any member of that 1\\u20132-element set.\\n- **Multiple-choice scoring.** MC Direction-A presents the frozen target alongside three matched-fame distractors sampled from the frozen targets of *other* parents in the sample (no distractors drawn from the same parent's other children). MC Direction-B presents the parent set (1\\u20132 entities; if 2, both are listed as separate options or as a single option depending on the format pre-registered below) with three matched-fame distractors drawn from other items' parent sets.\\n- **Normalization for textual scoring.**\\n  - Lowercase; strip diacritics; strip punctuation and whitespace; drop honorifics (\\\"Mr.\\\", \\\"Mrs.\\\", \\\"Dr.\\\", \\\"Sir\\\", \\\"Lady\\\", etc.).\\n  - Stage-name versus birth-name divergence: the accepted-aliases list is **pre-published as part of the stimulus metadata**, so scoring is deterministic. An item is credited if the response matches any alias in that list.\\n  - Final response parse: the first standalone name token (and an optional parenthetical alias) is the candidate answer; mismatched text after scoring is recorded but not penalized unless the candidate field is empty.\\n- **Open-ended primary; MC secondary.** All analyses are reported for open-ended; MC is reported as a ceiling-controlled secondary statistic only.\\n\\n## (iii) Statistical Analysis (Effect Size & Power)\\n\\n- **Unit of analysis:** the item (a single frozen Direction-A target child paired with the focal parent's 1\\u20132-element set for Direction B). Same items evaluated in both directions \\u21d2 paired design.\\n- **Primary statistic.** **One-sided McNemar's test**, \\u03b1 = .05, on the 2\\u00d72 of per-item outcomes across the two directions: A-correct-only / B-correct-only / both-correct / neither-correct. The 2\\u00d72 omits the both/neither diagonal from the discordant count as McNemar requires.\\n- **Two-sided robustness statistic.** The two-sided McNemar *p* is reported alongside every primary one-sided *p*.\\n- **Effect sizes.**\\n  - **Odds ratio** (exact conditional, with 95% CI) as a directional effect size in the discrete paired setting. The sign convention is fixed: OR > 1 means Direction-A accuracy exceeds Direction-B accuracy on the same item.\\n  - **Cohen's *d* (paired)** on the per-item accuracy difference |A \\u2212 B|, encoded as {\\u22121, 0, +1}, with bootstrap 95% CI.\\n- **Mixed-effects model.** Logit mixed-effects with fixed effects for Direction \\u00d7 Fame-band \\u00d7 Parent-gender (and a Direction \\u00d7 low-recency interaction for the sensitivity analysis), random intercepts for item, fitted via standard binomial GLMM machinery. Reported as an adjusted confirmation that the direction effect survives confounds.\\n- **Stratified reports.** The same McNemar + OR + *d* computed within (a) each fame band, (b) parent gender, (c) the low-recency sub-stratum.\\n- **Power / sample-size justification (paired binomial, one-sided).** Under a target odds ratio of 2.0 for the discordant cells (A-correct-only:B-correct-only) at moderate open-ended accuracy levels (~0.4\\u20130.7), McNemar reaches statistical power \\u2265 0.80 at \\u03b1 = .05 with N \\u2248 150 paired items. N = 80\\u2013100 is defensible for a strong effect; N = 150 is the design target and supports detection of more moderate directional asymmetries even after fame-band partitioning. Achieved power is reported post hoc regardless.\\n- **Multiple-comparisons handling.** No correction across the two formats (open-ended and MC) because MC is a single secondary ceiling-control check. Across fame-band / gender strata, results are reported descriptively; only the primary overall McNemar is the inferential decision statistic.\\n\\n## (iv) Feasibility\\n\\n- **Compute envelope.** 150 items \\u00d7 4 conditions (Direction \\u00d7 Format) \\u00d7 \\u22653 models \\u00d7 single-shot greedy decoding = \\u22651,800 LLM calls, plus control/MC-coordinate work. Single-shot/Greedy implies one generation per call; no sampling fan-out. This is well within a \\\"modest scale\\\" envelope for mixed open-weight + proprietary-API use.\\n- **Data volume.** Stimulus metadata (item_id, parent, frozen_target_child, alias list, fame-band, parent-gender, low-recency-flag, accepted-aliases list) is small text, on the order of tens of KB. Outputs are short (single token to a few tokens). Storage and analysis are trivially local-Python feasible.\\n- **Software dependencies.** Reproducible with standard public libraries; no GPU is required at design time, but if local open-weight models are evaluated in implementation a CPU-class inference path is feasible at the modest scale above.\\n- **Offline reproducibility.** Every stimulus decision is reproducible from a Wikidata JSON dump snapshot (offline fallback for the SPARQL endpoint) plus a Wikipedia revision-history dump (offline fallback for pageviews-based fame bands and the low-recency flag).\\n- **Model-version pinning convention.** Model candidates are described **generically** as \\u22653 distinct families with \\u22651 open-weight and \\u22651 proprietary-API representative from non-overlapping providers; specific version strings are not invented. At runtime, the exact `model_id` and version are logged and reported in-line in the methods/results; analysis treats versioned outputs as a hierarchical grouping (family / version / item).\\n- **Closed-set MC distractor pool.** Because distractors are sampled from frozen targets of *other* items in the same sample, the distractor pool scales with the item set; with N=150 the pool is generous and matched-fame sampling is straightforward.\\n\\n## Templates (verbatim)\\n\\n**System prompt (fixed, identical across all conditions):**\\n```\\nYou are a knowledgeable assistant answering factual questions. Respond with only the requested name, and no additional text.\\n```\\n\\n**Direction A \\u2014 open-ended (primary):**\\n```\\nName a child of {parent_full_name}.\\n```\\n\\n**Direction B \\u2014 open-ended (primary):**\\n```\\nName a parent of {child_full_name}.\\n```\\n\\n**Direction A \\u2014 multiple-choice (secondary, ceiling control):**\\n```\\nWhich of the following is a child of {parent_full_name}?\\n(A) {frozen_target_child_full_name}\\n(B) {distractor_1_full_name}\\n(C) {distractor_2_full_name}\\n(D) {distractor_3_full_name}\\n\\nReply with a single letter (A, B, C, or D).\\n```\\n\\n**Direction B \\u2014 multiple-choice (secondary, ceiling control):**\\n```\\nWhich of the following is a parent of {child_full_name}?\\n(A) {parent_option_full_name}\\n(B) {distractor_1_full_name}\\n(C) {distractor_2_full_name}\\n(D) {distractor_3_full_name}\\n\\nReply with a single letter (A, B, C, or D).\\n```\\n\\nNotes on template choice:\\n- Direction B with two biological parents: the parent option list is pre-published in the stimulus metadata as `{parent_set}`; in MC it is rendered as either a single composite option containing both names or two distinct options depending on a pre-registered rule (single-option preferred, since two-option choices introduce a separate \\\"select-correct-one\\\" confound). Free-recall Direction B credits any parent-set member.\\n- Consolidated per cell: one template per (Direction \\u00d7 Format) \\u2014 no per-item rewording.\\n- CoT condition (secondary sensitivity): the system prompt above is replaced with `You are a knowledgeable assistant. Think step by step, then give the requested name.` **only** in the CoT-on sensitivity arm; primary analysis uses the fixed default.\\n\\n## Evidence-to-be-reported Checklist\\n\\nPer model \\u00d7 direction \\u00d7 format:\\n- **Sample size** N of items evaluated.\\n- **Number correct** (raw tally).\\n- **Accuracy** (proportion correct), as %.\\n- **Wilson 95% confidence interval** on accuracy.\\n- **McNemar \\u03c7\\u00b2** (df = 1), one-sided *p* (primary) and two-sided *p* (robustness).\\n- **McNemar odds ratio** (exact conditional), with 95% CI; sign convention: OR > 1 \\u21d2 Direction-A > Direction-B.\\n- **Cohen's *d*** (paired) on per-item accuracy differences, with bootstrap 95% CI.\\n- **Item-level confusion exemplars** for the top-N disagreements (where Direction-A succeeds and Direction-B fails on the same item, and vice versa), drawn deterministically by descending |A\\u2212B|.\\n- **Stratified breakdowns** (each stratum re-runs McNemar + *d*):\\n  - by fame band (high / medium / low),\\n  - by parent gender (male / female),\\n  - by low-recency sub-stratum (sensitivity).\\n- **Mixed-effects logistic regression** results (Direction \\u00d7 Fame \\u00d7 Parent-gender; random intercept per item; the Direction \\u00d7 low-recency interaction in the sensitivity table) \\u2014 reported for transparency, not as the inferential decision.\\n- **Per-model version log**: `model_family`, `model_id`, and version string captured at runtime; analysis notes any version-fan-out.\\n\\n## Limitations and Threats to Validity (mitigations baked into the approved design)\\n\\n- **Memorization of celebrity content.** Mitigation already baked in: fame-band stratification (high/medium/low) is the primary control, reported descriptively alongside the inferential decision; a low-recency sub-stratum of pairs whose parent's biographic article was first edited after each model's declared knowledge cutoff is reserved and analyzed separately to detect memorization-driven results.\\n- **Link rot / drift in Wikipedia and Wikidata.** Mitigation baked in: the design reproduces entity curation offline from a Wikidata JSON dump; Wikipedia metadata used (article length, revision date, pageviews) is cached at stimulus-build time and shipped with the item file so item definitions do not drift between pilot and run.\\n- **Multi-child cardinality (asymmetry in set size of valid answers).** Mitigation already baked in: per the binding refinement above, every parent has exactly one frozen target child, deterministically chosen (alphabetical given name among English-Wikipedia-bearing children, stable seeded tiebreaker by item id), pre-published in the stimulus metadata; Direction-A free-recall is scored against that single target. MC distractors are drawn from other parents' frozen targets and matched on fame. Direction B uses the (1\\u20132 element) parent set as the universal reference set, transparently documented.\\n- **Prompt-sensitivity (small wording changes driving accuracy swings).** Mitigation baked in: both prompt formats are evaluated (open-ended primary, MC secondary), templates are verbatim above and shared across items within each cell, and the system prompt is fixed and reported; a CoT-on sensitivity arm is documented as secondary only and excluded from the primary decision.\\n- **Additional documented risks (not new design changes):** stage-name versus birth-name ambiguity is handled via the pre-published accepted-aliases list so that the scorer is deterministic; non-English aliases are excluded unless present on the Wikidata sitelink for English Wikipedia; refused-to-answer outputs are counted as misses and reported as a refusal-rate column so any blanket \\\"I don't know\\\" refusal can be distinguished from genuine directional misretrieval.\\n\\n## Feasibility Assessment (closing)\\n\\nThe design is feasible at modest scale: ~150 pairs \\u00d7 4 cells \\u00d7 \\u22653 models = a single-shot greedy decoding workload on the order of ~1,800 model calls producing short text outputs, well within ordinary API-rate and open-weight CPU/GPU inference budgets, with stimulus metadata, alias lists, and frozen targets small enough to inspect, version, and ship as a deterministic artefact. The largest open practical risks are (a) rate-limit coordination across \\u22653 model families, especially when batching proprietary-API calls, (b) Wikipedia/Wikidata link-rot between stimulus-build and evaluation runs (mitigated by offline reproducibility from a Wikidata dump and by caching all Wikipedia-derived features at build time), and (c) prompt-injection or stray-instruction risk when scraping biographical text for cross-checks (mitigated by treating scraped bios as inert source material only \\u2014 never included in any prompt and never used as scoring input \\u2014 and by using IMDb strictly as a human-review cross-check). All three risks are operational rather than design-level and require no change to the approved experimental choices.\", \"evidence\": \"Workspace inventory from the prior inspection round confirmed /workspace contained only task.json with the task metadata; no prior scripts, stimuli, seeds, notes, or analysis code exist. The design therefore had to be constructed from scratch. The binding resolutions from the researcher (Wikidata+Wikipedia+IMDb cross-check; biological-restricted sample with reverse-edge + surname/birth-spread corroboration; frozen single-target cardinality rule; fame-band stratification + low-recency sub-stratum; \\u22653 generic model families; greedy decoding with verbatim fixed system prompt and CoT-on sensitivity; ignore the question budget; normalize with pre-published alias list; one-sided McNemar primary with two-sided robustness) have been incorporated throughout sections (i)\\u2013(iv), the Templates block, the evidence-to-be-reported checklist, and the limitations/threats-to-validity section. The cardinality-fix refinement has been treated as binding: each parent is assigned one frozen target child via deterministic alphabetical + seeded-tiebreaker rule, Direction-A free-recall is scored against that single target only, and MC distractors are sampled from other parents' frozen targets fame-matched, neutralizing the parent-many-vs-child-few valid-answer asymmetry. Statistically the design relies on a within-item paired McNemar test (the natural paired-binomial test for directional asymmetry) with Wilson CIs on accuracy, exact conditional odds ratio with CI as directional effect size, paired Cohen's d as a continuous effect size, and a GLMM Direction \\u00d7 Fame \\u00d7 Parent-gender as adjusted confirmation. Power at N\\u2248150 paired items meets \\u22650.80 for OR\\u22482.0 under one-sided \\u03b1=.05.\", \"assumptions\": [], \"uncertainties\": []}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-de23822b-4537-4c2a-93c8-c6ecadc3b765", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 15}
